Perf

LOH

Measuring at the client, the dominant source of startup (or viewport teleport)
delay is now dynamically fetching the vectors, which involves the many
RTTs of the binary search. Instead of navigating the keyspace with log_2
RTTs, how about we navigate it with log_k for a much bigger k, using a B+-tree
shaped thing?

So instead of the current offset table / keys / values, we just have a B+-tree:
interior nodes have a list of pivot keys and child pointers. Leaf nodes
have a sorted key list and a value list, both of length k. So we still use
binsearch on the final node.

We can keep the page size at 64KiB. If a typical key is, say, 32B, that gives
us a fanout of ~2000! Much better than 2.

This is another breaking contract change, no legacy no regrets. Swap out the
nav-db builder and the core reader in one go.

We should still have a prefetch story: we can use the same key policy, walk
the b+tree at preproc to convert those keys into pages, and recommend prefetching
the list of touched pages.


- compress pages on disk. Somehow statically.

- do some more accounting. What's the bulk of the navdb being spent on?


