Refactoring & Cleanups

• cargo test -p app-core currently has 6 failing tests:

  - had_ops::tests::fixture_nav_kv_previews_append_entry_with_relationship_issue
  - had_ops::tests::generated_nav_kv_projects_kpae_vor_a_inserted_plan_ui_state
  - session::tests::replay_source_is_selectable_and_controls_playback_panel_visi
    bility
  - session::tests::row_action_activate_leg_works_after_on_plan_direct_to
  - session::tests::row_action_direct_to_targets_clicked_duplicate_row_uid
  - session::tests::session_projects_cdi_from_injected_guidance_geometry




  Longer-term, an even cleaner shape would be:

  - Android provides a generic byte reader / zip entry adapter
  - core owns the higher-level “open nav-db artifact” workflow
  - then core could be the one concluding “this artifact is unreadable”


a-ui is working on: aerobag/docs/refactor/core-platform-ui-boundary-audit.md


cleaning up web knowledge of core stuff:

  But I would not claim “entirely” without one caveat: web no longer knows the
  publication contract, but it still has a thin
  resolvePackageMemberUrl(packageId, memberPath) transport helper because some
  core APIs return product_id/path pairs for terrain/NEXRAD. That’s not parsing
  manifests or bundle roots, but it is still a platform-visible “package member”
  concept.

  So the honest grade:

  - Good: current_artifacts, bundle manifests, artifact roots, package lookup,
    and URL construction moved into core.
  - Good: web no longer has packageContract.ts, @current-artifacts, or
    package://.
  - Good: platform fetches opaque URLs from core and ingests bytes by resource
    id.
  - Still worth tightening later: APIs like terrain/NEXRAD should probably
    return core resource requests directly, so web never asks “resolve package
    member” at all.


